Couple of ways of implementing Interpreters:

[a] Switch-case: 
    while(inst = inst->next(inst)) {
       switch(inst) {
          case   i_add:   ... break;
	  case   i_sub:   ... break;
       }
    }   

 Assuming jump tables for the implementation, there are 3 branches here:
  * the while loop's backward jump
  * branch to check for inst not going beyond safe switch-case jmp table limits
  * the actual jmp to jmp table (which should have the code)


[b] Token Threading:
    while(inst = inst->next(inst)) {
       jmp codebase[inst] ; // perhaps some bound checking or maybe that could be done statically or 
       	    		    // during the bytecode verifier stage
    }

    * There is the while loop branch
    * its an indirect call so never a good thing

[c] Direct Threading:
    while(inst = inst->next(inst)) {
       jmp inst;
    }

    * There is the while loop branch
    * Just a direct branch so thats good

    As you can see, inst is the address of the implementation. Or perhaps you can add a fixed offset to it,
    it will still be a direct jmp.
    
    [Btw replace jmp above with call and you have Subroutine Threading]

[d] Inline Threading:
    Read David Mandelin's blog http://blog.mozilla.org/dmandelin/2008/08/27/inline-threading-tracemonkey-etc/
    Bottomline the idea is to create a separate buffer and copy inline the code for each bytecode into it
    and place a jmp to another dst at the end of the basic block.

    Thats it there are no dynamic dispatches whether direct or indirect beyond the last branch of each
    basic block.
    
    Conceptually you would imagine this is the fastest! 
